                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><strong>Food is about consuming chemistry, physics, and culture.</strong> The course "Food Chemistry" shares insights on <em>what to eat</em>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<em>how to eat</em> from the perspective of food's chemical nutrient components.</p>
<p>The focus on food varies across different eras, populations, and regions. Although hunger is largely absent today,&nbsp;<strong>how to eat healthily</strong>&nbsp;is indeed an issue worthy of everyone's attention. In recent years, groundbreaking research continuously emerging on the understanding of the relationship between the digestive system and health is&nbsp;<strong>directly linked to our physical well-being.</strong></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
